### Polymorphism and dynamic dispatch

**Subtype polymorphism** lets one piece of code operate on any object satisfying a declared interface. The mechanism is **dynamic dispatch**: the method body invoked is chosen from the receiver's *run-time* class, typically by an indirect jump through a virtual method table stored with the object.

This is what removes the conditional chain:

```
abstract class FeePolicy { abstract Money computeFee(Student s) }
class UndergraduatePolicy extends FeePolicy { ... }
class PostgraduatePolicy  extends FeePolicy { ... }
class ForeignStudentPolicy extends FeePolicy { ... }

// caller, unchanged forever:
Money total = policy.computeFee(student)
```

Adding a category adds a class. The call site is closed to modification and the hierarchy is open to extension.

**Overriding is not overloading.** Overloading selects among same-named methods with different parameter lists, and it is resolved **statically** from the declared types of the arguments. Overriding replaces a method body in a subtype, and it is resolved **dynamically** from the run-time class of the receiver. Conflating them produces genuinely surprising results.
